text:
samir_k: quick check — I'm updating terraform for a service that needs KMS decrypt but only when called from the pod (IRSA). I added a condition on the KMS key grant using EncryptionContext with `application = \"payments\"`. does that look sane?

mariab: can you paste the relevant snippets? (reaction :eyes:)

samir_k: sure — here's the key policy grant part i added:

```hcl
resource \"aws_kms_key\" \"payments\" {
  description = \"Payments key (used by payments svc)\"
}

resource \"aws_kms_grant\" \"payments_irsa\" {
  name            = \"payments-irsa-grant\"
  key_id          = aws_kms_key.payments.key_id
  grantee_principal = aws_iam_role.irsa_payments.arn
  operations      = [\"Decrypt\", \"Encrypt\"]
  constraints {
    encryption_context_equals = {
      application = \"payments\"
    }
  }
}
```

ajones: two things — 1) KMS grants don't persist across key rotations unless you handle them; 2) encryption context checks are powerful but the caller must pass exact context when calling kms:Decrypt. are you sure the runtime will include that context?

samir_k: runtime is a small helper in the svc that calls kms:Decrypt via aws sdk. i wired `EncryptionContext` in the decrypt call. but i'm worried about pod-to-role mapping: we create the IRSA role with the `sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` trust and the annotation on the k8s serviceAccount. tf snippet for role: 

```hcl
resource \"aws_iam_role\" \"irsa_payments\" {
  name = \"redwood-irsa-payments-${var.env}\"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = \"2012-10-17\"
    Statement = [{
      Effect = \"Allow\"
      Principal = { Federated = aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.oidc.arn }
      Action = \"sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity\"
      Condition = { StringEquals = { \"oidc.eks.example.com:sub\" = \"system:serviceaccount:payments:payments-sa\" } }
    }]
  })
}
```

katie_p: small nit — the condition key usually includes the provider host like `oidc.eks.${var.cluster_region}.amazonaws.com/id/${aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.oidc.url}` or the `:sub` claim shape. make sure it matches the actual OIDC `sub` claim for the token. we had a mismatch last month.

mariab: also check that the service account annotation includes the provider ARN exactly — otherwise the token won't be accepted. example: `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/redwood-irsa-payments-prod`

ajones: on the KMS side, if you constrain EncryptionContextEquals then any caller that omits or has different casing will get AccessDenied. SDKs normally pass contexts as map[string]string — casing matters. maybe use a less strict constraint: `kms:EncryptionContext:application` in key policy condition? or rely on principal-based grant without context?

samir_k: hm, we were trying to scope the key usage to this service only in case other roles exist in the account. suggestion: keep the grant to the IRSA role principal AND include an encryption context constraint. that should be fine if runtime always sets the context.

devops-bot: FYI — terraform plan shows: `~ aws_kms_grant.payments_irsa` (forces new resource) because `grantee_principal` changed. Apply will create new grant and revoke old one.

katie_p: how do you plan to roll? can you do a canary apply in dedicated-dev first? also confirm that pods on old nodes/old SA tokens won't break — they need the same annotations.

samir_k: plan is dev -> staging -> prod. i'll run canary in dev. question for ajones: any gotchas with grants being temporary after key rotation? iirc grants can be invalidated if key is scheduled rotation?

ajones: KMS key rotation (AWS-managed) doesn't delete grants but schedule/enable flags can affect. Also when you re-create a key resource (new key id) grants are gone. if you use alias rotation, consider using key policy to allow the role + limit by encryption context. grants are fine as long as key_id stays same.

mariab: one more test: run a pod with the SA and exec an aws kms decrypt CLI call with the same EncryptionContext to validate before rolling.

samir_k: will do. thanks for the quick reviews — i'll update the tf and ping when dev canary is up.

katie_p: thanks — post the plan output here once you run it so we can eyeball diffs. :thumbsup:
